/* DEEP NOTES — O Level sciences & mathematics & islamiyat (expansion layer; overrides EV_HUB notes when present) */
window.EV_HUB_DEEP = {
"olevel/chem5070":[
{t:"1. Experimental techniques & purity",b:"<ul><li><b>Purity test:</b> a pure solid melts sharply at one temperature; impurities LOWER the melting point and WIDEN the range. Pure liquids boil constantly.</li><li><b>Choosing separation:</b> filtration (insoluble solid from liquid), crystallisation (heat-sensitive dissolved solid — slow cooling gives big crystals), evaporation (salt from brine), simple distillation (solvent from solution, e.g. seawater → water), fractional distillation (miscible liquids by b.p. — crude oil, ethanol–water), separating funnel (immiscible liquids), chromatography (dyes/amino acids).</li><li><b>Chromatography:</b> Rf = distance of spot ÷ distance of solvent front (always ≤ 1, measured from baseline pencil line — never ink). Identical Rf in same solvent ⇒ possibly same substance.</li><li><b>Titration apparatus discipline:</b> rinse burette with the solution it will hold, pipette with the solution it measures, conical flask with distilled water only. Read burette to 0.05 cm³ at eye level (meniscus). Indicators: methyl orange or phenolphthalein for strong acid–alkali.</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. The mole & stoichiometry",b:"<ul><li>Core conversions: n = m/M · n = c × V(dm³) · n = V(gas)/24 dm³ at r.t.p. · N = n × 6.02 × 10²³.</li><li><b>Reacting masses:</b> write the equation → moles of known → use the mole ratio → convert to the wanted quantity. This 4-step chain answers EVERY mass/mass, mass/volume and titration question.</li><li><b>% yield</b> = actual ÷ theoretical × 100. Yield is lost by: incomplete reaction, side reactions, transfer losses, purification losses.</li><li><b>% purity</b> = mass of pure substance ÷ mass of impure sample × 100.</li><li><b>Empirical formula:</b> % ÷ A<sub>r</sub> → ÷ smallest → whole numbers (multiply ×2 if .5 appears). Molecular formula = (empirical)ₙ where n = M ÷ empirical mass.</li><li><b>Concentration conversions:</b> mol/dm³ = g/dm³ ÷ M. Dilutions: c₁V₁ = c₂V₂.</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Atomic structure & bonding",b:"<ul><li>Proton number = electrons (neutral atom); nucleon number = p + n. Isotopes = same protons, different neutrons: ¹²C (standard), ¹⁴C (carbon dating), ²H (heavy hydrogen), ²³⁵U (fuel).</li><li><b>Ionic bonding:</b> electron TRANSFER metal→non-metal; giant lattice; conducts when molten/aqueous (mobile ions); high m.p.; brittle (like ions repel when layers shift).</li><li><b>Covalent:</b> SHARING. Simple molecular (H₂O, CO₂, I₂): low m.p., non-conductors, often soluble in organic solvents. Giant covalent (diamond: 4 bonds, hardest, non-conductor; graphite: 3 bonds + delocalised electrons → conducts, slippery, used in electrodes/pencils; SiO₂ similar to diamond).</li><li><b>Metallic:</b> cations in a sea of delocalised electrons → conducts, malleable (layers slide without breaking — electrons glue them).</li><li>Dot-and-cross diagrams REQUIRED for NaCl, MgO, CaCl₂, H₂, H₂O, CO₂, CH₄, NH₃.</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Redox, electrolysis & electricity",b:"<ul><li>OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain (of electrons). Oxidising agent accepts electrons (is itself reduced).</li><li>Electrolytes: molten/aqueous ionic compounds, acids, alkalis — NOT sugar, ethanol, pure water (very weak).</li><li><b>Discharge rules:</b> cation of a metal BELOW H in the reactivity series is discharged; H⁺ is discharged from aqueous solutions of metals ABOVE H. Anions: halide discharged (except very dilute solutions → O₂), otherwise OH⁻ → O₂, SO₄²⁻/NO₃⁻ never discharged.</li><li><b>Brine (conc. NaCl):</b> cathode H₂, anode Cl₂, solution becomes NaOH — the chlor-alkali industry.</li><li><b>Active electrodes:</b> Cu anode dissolves (Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻); used in electroplating (spoon as cathode) and copper refining.</li><li>Quantitative: moles of e⁻ = Q ÷ 96 500 C; e.g. Cu²⁺ needs 2e⁻ per atom.</li></ul>"},
{t:"5. Chemical energetics",b:"<ul><li>Exothermic (ΔH negative, heat released): combustion, neutralisation, respiration, freezing, condensation. Endothermic: thermal decomposition (CaCO₃), photosynthesis, dissolving NH₄Cl, melting, boiling.</li><li>Energy profile diagrams: reactants→peak = activation energy E<sub>a</sub>; difference reactants→products = ΔH. Label BOTH arrows.</li><li>Bond energy estimate: ΔH = Σ(bonds broken) − Σ(bonds formed). Breaking = endothermic, making = exothermic.</li><li>Calorimetry: q = mcΔθ; assume 1 cm³ water = 1 g, c = 4.18 J/g°C; heat loss → measured ΔH smaller in magnitude than true value.</li><li>Fuel choices compare: energy per gram, availability, pollution, storage.</li></ul>"},
{t:"6. Rate of reaction",b:"<ul><li>Collision theory: reaction needs collisions with energy ≥ E<sub>a</sub> and correct orientation.</li><li>Factors: concentration/pressure (more particles per volume → more frequent collisions), surface area (powder ≫ lumps), temperature (T +10°C ≈ doubles rate — more collisions AND more exceed E<sub>a</sub>), catalyst (alternative route, LOWER E<sub>a</sub>; unchanged chemically at the end; enzymes are biological catalysts).</li><li>Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution: catalyst does NOT shift the curve or increase temperature — it lowers the E<sub>a</sub> line, so more particles qualify.</li><li>Graphs: gradient = rate; steepest at start (reactants most concentrated); curves level off when a reactant finishes. SAME final volume with/without catalyst — catalyst only changes SPEED.</li><li>Measuring rate: gas syringe volume vs time, mass loss (CO₂ escapes), cross-disappearance (sulfur precipitate), titrating samples vs time.</li></ul>"},
{t:"7. Acids, bases & salts",b:"<ul><li>Acid = H⁺ donor; alkali = soluble base (OH⁻ in water). pH < 7 acid, > 7 alkali; universal indicator colour scale; strong (HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃) fully ionise; weak (ethanoic, carbonic) partially.</li><li>Reactions: acid + metal → salt + H₂ · acid + carbonate → salt + H₂O + CO₂ · acid + alkali → salt + H₂O (neutralisation) · acid + oxide/hydroxide → salt + water.</li><li><b>Soluble salt preparation:</b> (a) titration for Na⁺/K⁺/NH₄⁺ salts (exact quantities, indicator removed by evaporation to near-dryness before adding salt-forming acid); (b) add EXCESS insoluble reactant to acid, filter excess, crystallise filtrate.</li><li><b>Insoluble salts:</b> precipitation — mix two soluble salts containing the wanted ions, filter, wash with distilled water, dry (e.g. BaSO₄, AgCl, PbSO₄, CaCO₃).</li><li>Tests: NH₄⁺ (warm with NaOH → NH₃ turns damp red litmus blue) · Fe²⁺ dirty-green ppt / Fe³⁺ reddish-brown / Cu²⁺ pale-blue with NaOH(aq) · Cl⁻ white ppt with AgNO₃/HNO₃ · SO₄²⁻ white ppt with BaCl₂/HCl · CO₃²⁻ effervescence with acid (CO₂ → limewater milky).</li></ul>"},
{t:"8. The Periodic Table",b:"<ul><li>Group I (alkali metals): soft, shiny→tarnish instantly, float, melt more easily down the group, react with water → hydroxide + H₂; reactivity INCREASES down (outer electron further from nucleus, more shielding → lost more easily).</li><li>Group VII (halogens): coloured (F₂ pale yellow, Cl₂ green, Br₂ red-brown, I₂ grey-black), diatomic, reactivity DECREASES down (harder to gain an electron). Displacement: Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂ (a more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive one from its salt).</li><li>Group 0 (noble gases): full outer shell → unreactive; uses: Ar (welding, bulbs), Ne (advertising signs), He (balloons, diving gas, coolant), Kr/Xe (lamps, lasers).</li><li>Transition metals (between II and III): high m.p., hard, variable oxidation states (Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺, Cu⁺/Cu²⁺), COLOURED compounds (Cu²⁺ blue, Fe²⁺ green, Fe³⁺ yellow-brown), catalysts (Fe — Haber; Ni — hydrogenation; V₂O₅ — Contact; Pt — car exhaust), form complex ions.</li></ul>"},
{t:"9. Metals, extraction & rusting",b:"<ul><li>Reactivity series: K Na Ca Mg Al Zn Fe Pb (H) Cu Ag Au. Position decides: reaction with water/steam/dilute acid, displacement, extraction method.</li><li><b>Extraction:</b> K–Al: electrolysis of molten ores (huge energy); Zn–Fe: reduction with carbon/carbon monoxide in a blast furnace; below Fe: roasting in air suffices.</li><li><b>Blast furnace:</b> C + O₂ → CO₂; CO₂ + C → 2CO; Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂; limestone decomposes → CaO + CO₂; CaO + SiO₂ → CaSiO₃ slag (floats on iron, protects it; used for roads). Iron from the furnace = pig iron (brittle, 4% C) → steels by removing carbon and adding alloying metals.</li><li><b>Steel types:</b> mild steel (buildings, cars — cheap, strong), stainless steel (Cr + Ni: cutlery, surgical tools — rustproof), high-carbon steel (drills, hammers — hard but brittle).</li><li><b>Rusting</b> needs BOTH water and oxygen. Prevention: paint, oil/grease, plastic coating, galvanising (zinc coating — sacrificial even when scratched), sacrificial blocks (Mg/Zn on ships/pipelines), tin-plating (NOT sacrificial — if scratched, iron rusts faster).</li><li>Alloys: brass (Cu+Zn), bronze (Cu+Sn), solder (Pb+Sn, low m.p.), duralumin (Al alloy, aircraft — light + strong).</li></ul>"},
{t:"10. Air, water & sulfur",b:"<ul><li>Air composition (~vol): N₂ 78%, O₂ 21%, Ar 0.9%, CO₂ 0.04% (rising). Separated by fractional distillation of liquefied air.</li><li>Combustion of fuels: complete (plenty of O₂) → CO₂ + H₂O; incomplete (limited O₂) → CO (toxic, odourless, binds haemoglobin) or soot C.</li><li><b>Water treatment:</b> screening → coagulation/flocculation (alum) → sedimentation → sand filtration → chlorination (kills microbes; debate: residual protection vs by-products). Fluoridation where practised.</li><li>Hard water contains Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺: temporary hardness = hydrogencarbonates (removed by BOILING → CaCO₃ precipitate), permanent = sulfates/chlorides (removed by ion-exchange or washing soda Na₂CO₃, not boiling). Hardness: wastes soap (scum), BUT Ca²⁺ reduces heart disease, tastes better, no lead dissolution from pipes.</li><li>Test for water: anhydrous CuSO₄ white → blue, or anhydrous CoCl₂ blue → pink.</li><li>Sulfur: mined by Frasch process; S + O₂ → SO₂ (pungent, acid rain precursor); Contact process: 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃ (V₂O₅ catalyst, 450°C, ~2 atm) → oleum → H₂SO₄. Uses: fertilisers, batteries, detergents, paints.</li><li>Acid rain (SO₂, NOₓ from burning fossil fuels): pH < 5.6, corrodes limestone/iron, kills fish, leaches soil nutrients. Remedies: scrubbers, catalytic converters, cleaner fuels.</li></ul>"},
{t:"11. Organic chemistry I — hydrocarbons",b:"<ul><li>Homologous series = same general formula, similar chemistry, gradual physical trend, successive members differ by CH₂ (14 u). Functional group = atom/group deciding chemistry.</li><li><b>Alkanes</b> CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ (saturated, single bonds): generally unreactive except BURNING (CO₂ + H₂O) and SUBSTITUTION with Cl₂/Br₂ in sunlight (free-radical chain). Methane from anaerobic decay (biogas, landfill); alkanes from crude oil (fractional distillation) and cracking.</li><li><b>Cracking:</b> big alkane → smaller alkane + ALKENE (+ H₂ sometimes); conditions: 400–700°C, alumina/silica catalyst. Why crack: petrol demand > supply of light fractions; alkenes are valuable feedstock.</li><li><b>Alkenes</b> CₙH₂ₙ (one C=C): made by cracking; TEST = decolourise bromine water (orange→colourless) / acidified KMnO₄ (purple→colourless). Addition reactions: +H₂ (Ni catalyst → alkane), +steam (H₃PO₄ catalyst → alcohol), +HX, +X₂.</li><li>Polymerisation: n CH₂=CH₂ → poly(ethene) — addition polymer; repeat unit drawn with continuation bonds through brackets.</li></ul>"},
{t:"12. Organic chemistry II — oxygen compounds",b:"<ul><li><b>Alcohols</b> CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH: ethanol by FERMENTATION of sugars (yeast enzymes ~30°C, anaerobic, then fractional distillation) or hydration of ethene. Alcohols burn, react with Na (→H₂), oxidise on standing with air/bacteria → acids.</li><li><b>Carboxylic acids</b> CₙH₂ₙ₊₁COOH: weak acids (partial ionisation, pH 3–5, still fizz with carbonates — slower than mineral acids). Ethanoic acid from oxidation of ethanol (vinegar).</li><li><b>Esters</b> RCOOR′: acid + alcohol ⇌ ester + water (conc. H₂SO₄ catalyst); sweet/fruity smell; uses: perfumes, flavourings, solvents (ethyl ethanoate), fats/oils are esters of glycerol.</li><li>Saponification: fat/oil + NaOH → soap (sodium salt of fatty acid) + glycerol. Soapless detergents from petroleum sulfonates.</li><li>Isomerism: same molecular formula, different structure — draw chain, position and functional-group isomers from C₄ upwards.</li><li>Identification flow: bromine water (unsaturation), Na₂CO₃ (acid: effervesces), ester smell test, burning characteristics.</li></ul>"},
{t:"13. Equilibria & industrial chemistry",b:"<ul><li>Reversible reactions: ⇌, products can convert back. Dynamic equilibrium (closed container): forward rate = backward rate, concentrations constant.</li><li>Le Chatelier: change concentration/pressure/temperature → system shifts to COUNTERACT. Pressure: shifts to side with FEWER gas molecules. Temperature: raise T → shifts in ENDOTHERMIC direction. Catalyst: NO shift — reaches equilibrium FASTER.</li><li><b>Haber process</b> N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ (ΔH = −92 kJ/mol): Fe catalyst, ~450°C, ~200 atm. Compromise conditions: low T favours yield but is slow; high P favours yield but is expensive/dangerous.</li><li><b>Contact process</b> 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃: V₂O₅ catalyst, 450°C, ~2 atm.</li><li>Fertilisers: N (ammonium salts, urea — from Haber NH₃), P (from phosphate rock + H₂SO₄), K (potassium chloride/sulfate). Excess fertiliser → eutrophication: algal bloom → decomposition depletes O₂ → aquatic death.</li><li>Green chemistry ideas: atom economy, catalysts replacing stoichiometric reagents, renewable feedstocks, non-toxic solvents.</li></ul>"}],
"olevel/phys5054":[
{t:"1. Measurements, scalars & vectors",b:"<ul><li>Base quantities & SI units: length m, mass kg, time s, current A, temperature K. Prefixes: n µ m c k M G.</li><li>Scalar (magnitude only): speed, distance, mass, energy, temperature. Vector (magnitude + direction): displacement, velocity, acceleration, force, momentum, weight.</li><li>Add vectors by scale drawing (parallelogram/tip-to-tail) or components: vₓ = v cos θ, v_y = v sin θ. A 30 N horizontal + 40 N vertical force = 50 N resultant (3-4-5 triangle).</li><li>Measuring instruments: metre rule (1 mm), micrometer screw gauge (0.01 mm — rotate ratchet 3 clicks), vernier calipers (0.1 mm). Zero error correction: subtract the zero reading.</li><li>Precaution culture: repeat readings → mean; avoid parallax (eye perpendicular to scale); for oscillations, time 20 swings and divide (reaction-time error ÷20).</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Kinematics & graphs",b:"<ul><li>Speed = distance/time (scalar); velocity = displacement/time (vector). Acceleration = Δv/Δt, units m/s².</li><li><b>Distance–time graph:</b> gradient = speed; curved = changing speed; horizontal = stationary. <b>Speed–time graph:</b> gradient = acceleration; AREA under graph = distance travelled; below-axis area = reverse travel.</li><li>SUVAT (constant acceleration ONLY): v = u + at · s = ut + ½at² · v² = u² + 2as · s = ½(u+v)t. Free fall: a = g = 9.8–10 m/s² downward, u may be 0.</li><li>Worked pattern: list u, v, a, s, t with signs (up = +) → choose the equation containing the unknown and knowns → solve → sanity-check the sign.</li><li>Terminal velocity: falling object accelerates until drag = weight (net force 0) → constant velocity; depends on shape/area (parachute), occurs quickly for low mass:area (raindrop).</li><li>Projectile motion: horizontal velocity CONSTANT; vertical velocity changes with g — solve the two directions independently.</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Dynamics — Newton's laws & momentum",b:"<ul><li>1st law (inertia): constant velocity (or rest) unless a resultant force acts. Mass = inertia measure.</li><li>2nd law: F = ma (resultant force); weight W = mg. 1 N = force giving 1 kg an acceleration of 1 m/s².</li><li>3rd law: action–reaction pairs are EQUAL, OPPOSITE, and act on DIFFERENT bodies (swimmer pushes water back — water pushes swimmer forward; rocket pushes gas down — gas pushes rocket up).</li><li>Everyday applications: seatbelts/airbags (increase stopping time → reduce force, F = Δp/Δt), crumple zones, head restraints.</li><li><b>Momentum</b> p = mv (kg·m/s), vector. Conservation: total momentum before = after collision/explosion (no external resultant force). Elastic: KE also conserved; inelastic: KE lost to heat/sound/deformation.</li><li>Impulse = FΔt = Δp — cricket fielder draws hands back, egg-catching foam, high-jump landing mats: all lengthen Δt to reduce F.</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Forces, moments & equilibrium",b:"<ul><li>Moment of force = force × perpendicular distance from pivot (N·m). Principle of moments (equilibrium): sum of clockwise moments = sum of anticlockwise moments.</li><li>Centre of gravity: point where weight appears to act; find by plumb-line method for irregular lamina. Stable equilibrium: CG rises on tilting and falls back (wide base, low CG); unstable: CG falls away.</li><li>Conditions for equilibrium: ΣF = 0 AND Σmoments = 0.</li><li>Friction: useful (walking, braking, grip) & wasteful (overcome by lubricants, rollers, streamlining). Limiting friction vs kinetic friction.</li><li>Pressure = F/A (Pa). Increase A → decrease pressure (snowshoes, tractor tyres, drawing pins the other way). Hydraulic systems: P = F₁/A₁ = F₂/A₂ → force multiplication (car brakes, jacks) with incompressible liquid.</li><li>Hooke's law: extension ∝ load up to the limit of proportionality: F = kx. Beyond elastic limit → permanent deformation; breaking point next. Energy stored = ½Fx = area under F–x graph.</li></ul>"},
{t:"5. Work, energy & power",b:"<ul><li>Work = force × distance moved in force direction (J); W = Fs; work against friction → heat.</li><li>Energy forms: KE = ½mv², GPE = mgh, elastic, thermal, chemical, nuclear, electrical, sound, light. Conservation: transforms, never created/destroyed.</li><li>Power = work/time = energy/time (W); P = Fv for vehicles. Efficiency = useful output ÷ total input × 100%.</li><li>Energy resources: renewable (solar, wind, hydro, tidal, geothermal, biomass) vs non-renewable (fossil, nuclear). Compare by cost, reliability, pollution, location, startup cost. Solar cell → electricity directly; solar panel → heat water; wind → kinetic → electrical.</li><li>Degraded energy: every conversion loses some to heat — why efficiency < 100% always.</li></ul>"},
{t:"6. Thermal physics",b:"<ul><li>States & particle model: solid (fixed positions, vibrate), liquid (slide past), gas (free, fast). Changes: melting, boiling/evaporation, condensation, sublimation (camphor, CO₂), deposition.</li><li><b>Evaporation vs boiling:</b> evaporation below b.p., only surface, any temperature, cooling effect (fastest molecules escape → average KE falls); boiling at b.p., throughout liquid, constant T.</li><li><b>Specific heat capacity:</b> E = mcΔθ. Water's huge c (4200 J/kg°C) → coolant, climate moderation. Experiment: heater method E = VIt, with insulation + lid + stir + repeat.</li><li><b>Latent heat:</b> E = ml (fusion ~334 kJ/kg water; vaporisation ~2260 kJ/kg — large → steam burns worse than boiling water). Temperature constant during change of state — energy breaks/makes bonds not speed.</li><li>Conduction: metals — free electrons + lattice vibrations; convection — fluids only, by density differences; radiation — infrared EM waves, no medium, dull black surfaces best emitters/absorbers (vacuum flask: silvered walls, vacuum, insulating support, stopper).</li><li>Thermal expansion: gaps in bridges/railway tracks, bimetallic strips (thermostats); water anomaly: ice floats (4°C densest) — aquatic life survives winter.</li></ul>"},
{t:"7. Waves & sound",b:"<ul><li>Wave = energy transfer without net matter movement. Transverse (light, ripples, EM): oscillation ⊥ travel. Longitudinal (sound, compression in springs): oscillation ∥ travel.</li><li>v = fλ; f = 1/T. Sound: ~330–340 m/s in air, faster in water/solids; needs a medium — NOT in vacuum. Light: 3 × 10⁸ m/s in vacuum.</li><li>Reflection: angle i = angle r; plane mirrors → virtual/same size/same distance behind. Refraction: speed change → bending; into denser → towards normal; n = sin i/sin r; apparent depth < real depth.</li><li>Diffraction: spreading through gaps/obstacles — greatest when gap ≈ λ. Long waves (radio) diffract around hills; why you hear round corners.</li><li>Sound: compression/rarefaction; pitch ∝ frequency; loudness ∝ amplitude; quality ∝ waveform. Ultrasound > 20 kHz: sonar, fetal scanning, cleaning. Echo: distance = v × t/2.</li><li>EM spectrum order: radio, microwave, infrared, visible, UV, X-ray, gamma. Uses & dangers of each band.</li></ul>"},
{t:"8. Light & optics",b:"<ul><li>Thin-lens ray rules: parallel ray → through far focus; focal ray → emerges parallel; central ray → straight. Real images: inverted, projectable; virtual: upright, cannot.</li><li>Camera: real, inverted, diminished between F and 2F. Magnifying glass: object inside F → virtual, upright, enlarged. Projector: object between F and 2F → real, inverted, enlarged beyond 2F.</li><li>Total internal reflection: light inside denser medium beyond the critical angle reflects totally: sin c = 1/n. Applications: optical fibres (endoscopy, broadband), periscopes, prisms in binoculars, sparkling diamonds.</li><li>Colours: white light disperses through a prism (red deviated least, violet most). Primary colours of light: red + green + blue = white.</li><li>Light travels in straight lines (pinhole camera; shadows; eclipses); speed 3 × 10⁸ m/s in vacuum.</li></ul>"},
{t:"9. Electricity & circuits",b:"<ul><li>Q = It (coulombs = amps × seconds). Conventional current + to −; electrons flow the other way.</li><li>EMF = total energy per coulomb from the source; p.d. = energy per coulomb across a component; both in volts. V = IR (Ohm's law — ohmic conductors only).</li><li>Series: same current; V splits; R_total = R₁+R₂+… Parallel: same V; current splits; 1/R = Σ1/Rᵢ. Household circuits are PARALLEL (each appliance full mains V; switches on live wire; fuses in live wire).</li><li>Power: P = VI = I²R = V²/R. Energy: E = Pt (kWh = kW × h; 1 kWh = 3.6 MJ — the 'unit' on bills). Cost = units × price.</li><li>Fuse rating just above normal current (P = VI: 3 A covers < 720 W at 240 V). Fuse MELTS when too much current flows (short circuit or overload). Earth wire: metal-case fault → large current to earth → fuse blows. Double insulation needs no earth.</li><li>Circuit breakers & RCDs: faster, resettable. Mains: 220–240 V AC, 50 Hz.</li></ul>"},
{t:"10. Magnetism & electromagnetism",b:"<ul><li>Bar magnet: N and S poles; like repel; field lines N→S outside; steel keeps magnetism (permanent), soft iron temporary (electromagnets).</li><li>Compass needle points along the field; Earth's field enables navigation.</li><li>Current → circular field (right-hand grip rule); solenoid → bar-magnet-like field; soft-iron core → ELECTROMAGNET, strength ↑ with current, turns/cm, core quality. Uses: bells, relays, cranes, Maglev.</li><li>Motor effect: F = BIL (max when perpendicular); direction by Fleming's LEFT-hand rule. Motor: coil + commutator reverses current every half-turn → continuous rotation; faster with stronger magnets, more current, more turns.</li><li>Electromagnetic induction: emf induced when conductor cuts field lines — motion or changing field; size ↑ with speed, field strength, turns. Lenz's law: induced current OPPOSES the change.</li><li>Generator: coil in field → alternating emf; slip rings (AC) vs split-ring commutator (DC). Transformer: V_p/V_s = N_p/N_s; step-up for transmission (less I²R heat loss); national grid: power station → step-up → grid → step-down → consumers.</li></ul>"},
{t:"11. Atomic & nuclear physics",b:"<ul><li>Atom: dense nucleus (protons + neutrons, most of mass) + electrons in shells. Rutherford's gold-foil scattering: most α passed straight (atom mostly empty), few deflected (small positive nucleus), very few bounced back (nucleus massive).</li><li>Isotopes: same protons, different neutrons — ¹⁴C dating, ⁶⁰Co treatment, ²³⁵U fuel.</li><li>Radioactive decay is RANDOM and SPONTANEOUS. Radiations: α = helium nucleus — strongly ionising, stopped by paper; β = electron — mm Al; γ = EM wave — needs lead/concrete, undeflected by fields.</li><li>Decay equations: α → A −4, Z −2; β → A same, Z +1; γ → no change. Conserve mass & atomic numbers.</li><li>Half-life: time for half the nuclei to decay — CONSTANT per isotope. Graph method: halve a value, read the time gap. Uses by half-life: smoke alarm ²⁴¹Am, tracers ⁹⁹ᵐTc, dating ¹⁴C (5730 y).</li><li>Fission: ²³⁵U + neutron → fragments + 2–3 neutrons + ~200 MeV; chain reaction controlled by moderator & control rods. Fusion: 2H + 3H → ⁴He + n — powers the Sun; needs ~10⁷°C.</li><li>Safety: background radiation sources (cosmic, granite, radon, medical); shielding/distance/time; waste deep-stored; irradiated food is NOT radioactive.</li></ul>"},
{t:"12. Exam technique for 5054",b:"<ul><li>Paper 1 (MCQ): 40 questions, 1 h → 90 s each. Eliminate absurd options; watch unit traps (cm vs m, g vs kg, kW vs W).</li><li>Paper 2: definitions word-perfect ('the rate of change of momentum'); explain questions want the mechanism (particles/electrons/forces named); calculations need formula → substitution → answer + unit, 3 s.f.</li><li>Definitions bank: acceleration, average speed, moment, pressure, work, power, efficiency, half-life, isotope, frequency, wavelength, critical angle, centre of gravity.</li><li>Common mark-scheme phrases: 'because the centre of gravity is lowered/rises', 'electrons are free to move', 'molecules gain kinetic energy and escape from the surface', 'rate of change of momentum', 'induced emf when lines of force are cut'.</li><li>Check the unit asked (kJ vs J, g vs kg, minutes vs seconds); give vectors a direction when the question says 'velocity' or 'force'.</li></ul>"},
{t:"13. Definitions drill — say them cold",b:"<ul><li><b>Speed:</b> distance travelled per unit time. <b>Velocity:</b> speed in a stated direction. <b>Acceleration:</b> rate of change of velocity. <b>Momentum:</b> mass × velocity.</li><li><b>Weight:</b> force of gravity on a mass (W = mg). <b>Density:</b> mass per unit volume. <b>Pressure:</b> force per unit area.</li><li><b>Moment:</b> force × perpendicular distance from pivot. <b>Work:</b> force × distance moved in the direction of the force. <b>Power:</b> work done per unit time.</li><li><b>Frequency:</b> waves passing a point per second. <b>Wavelength:</b> distance between consecutive in-phase points. <b>Amplitude:</b> maximum displacement from rest.</li><li><b>Critical angle:</b> angle of incidence (in denser medium) giving 90° refraction. <b>Half-life:</b> time for half the radioactive nuclei to decay / activity to halve.</li><li><b>Latent heat of vaporisation:</b> energy to change 1 kg of liquid to gas at constant temperature. <b>Specific heat capacity:</b> energy to raise 1 kg by 1°C.</li></ul>"},
{t:"14. Calculation templates",b:"<ul><li><b>SUVAT:</b> list knowns with signs → select equation with exactly one unknown → solve → check sign makes sense.</li><li><b>Momentum collision:</b> total p before = total p after, each side Σmv with signs.</li><li><b>Moments:</b> draw pivot, mark all forces & perpendicular distances, CW = ACW.</li><li><b>Hooke/graph:</b> k = gradient (F/x); energy = ½Fx = area under line.</li><li><b>Calorimetry:</b> E = mcΔθ (or ml); electrical heater: E = VIt. Precautions: lid + insulation, stir, repeat.</li><li><b>Circuits:</b> redraw parallel → equivalent R → series sum → total current → back-fill branches. Power: P = VI; cost: kWh × rate.</li><li><b>Waves:</b> v = fλ with unit conversion; echo distance = vt/2; T = 1/f.</li><li><b>Half-life from table:</b> count ratio (e.g. 800→200 = two halvings); t½ = elapsed ÷ halvings.</li></ul>"}],
"olevel/bio5090":[
{t:"1. Cell structure & microscopy",b:"<ul><li>Animal cell: nucleus (controls, contains DNA), cytoplasm (reactions), cell membrane (controls entry/exit, partially permeable), mitochondria (aerobic respiration). Plant adds: cell wall (cellulose, fully permeable, support), chloroplasts (photosynthesis), permanent vacuole (sap — keeps cell rigid).</li><li>Specialised cells: root hair (long extension → large surface for absorption), red blood (biconcave → large SA, no nucleus → more haemoglobin), sperm (tail, mitochondria), xylem (dead hollow lignified tubes — water transport + support), nerve (long axon), guard cells (kidney-shaped, stomata control).</li><li>Microscopy: magnification = image size ÷ actual size; 1 mm = 1000 µm; 1 µm = 1000 nm. Convert BEFORE calculating. Electron microscopes: higher magnification/resolution, but specimens dead & in vacuum.</li><li>Drawing rules: sharp pencil, continuous thin lines, no shading, label lines straight with a ruler.</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Movement in & out of cells",b:"<ul><li><b>Diffusion:</b> net movement from high → low concentration; passive. Faster with bigger gradient, higher temperature, larger surface area. Examples: O₂/CO₂ in lungs & leaves, glucose from gut, mineral ions into roots.</li><li><b>Osmosis:</b> diffusion of WATER only, across a partially permeable membrane, from dilute → concentrated solution. Experiments: potato strips in sucrose — mass/length change; Visking tubing models the gut wall.</li><li><b>Active transport:</b> movement AGAINST the gradient, needs carrier proteins + ATP (from respiration). Examples: mineral ions into root hairs, glucose from gut into blood, kidney reabsorption. Killed cells stop absorbing → proves ATP needed.</li><li>Comparing: diffusion needs no membrane proteins or energy; active transport stops at low temperature or with metabolic poisons.</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Biological molecules & enzymes",b:"<ul><li>Carbohydrates: starch TEST = iodine → blue-black; glucose = Benedict's heated → brick-red; sucrose = non-reducing (hydrolyse first with dilute HCl).</li><li>Proteins: biuret → purple. Fats: ethanol emulsion → cloudy white; or grease spot on paper.</li><li>Enzymes: protein catalysts; active site fits substrate ('lock and key'); unchanged & reusable, highly specific.</li><li>Rate factors: temperature — rises to optimum (~37°C) then DENATURED (bonds break, active site shape lost PERMANENTLY); pH — pepsin ~2, amylase ~7, extremes denature; concentration — faster until saturation.</li><li>Experiments: amylase + starch sampled with iodine; liver catalase + H₂O₂ (frothing, glowing splint); water baths control temperature.</li><li>Denaturation is NOT melting — losing shape, usually irreversible.</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Plant nutrition — photosynthesis",b:"<ul><li>6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ (light energy captured by chlorophyll in chloroplasts). Glucose used for: respiration, starch storage, cellulose walls, amino acids (with nitrates), oils.</li><li><b>Leaf adaptations:</b> broad flat lamina (large SA), thin (short diffusion distance), palisade mesophyll packed with chloroplasts, stomata (mostly lower epidermis), air spaces, veins, waxy cuticle.</li><li>Limiting factors: light intensity, CO₂ concentration, temperature — the factor in shortest supply limits the rate. Greenhouse tricks: extra CO₂, artificial light, heating.</li><li><b>Starch test:</b> destarch in the dark 24 h FIRST · boil in water · boil in ethanol (water bath — flammable!) to remove chlorophyll · rinse · iodine → blue-black where starch. Variegated leaf: only green parts positive (chlorophyll needed). Masked leaf: light needed. Soda-lime bag: CO₂ needed.</li><li>Starch is the store because glucose is soluble (would disturb osmosis) and reactive.</li></ul>"},
{t:"5. Human nutrition & the gut",b:"<ul><li>Balanced diet: carbohydrates (energy), proteins (growth/repair), fats (energy store, insulation), vitamins, minerals, water, fibre. Deficiencies: vitamin C → scurvy; vitamin D/Ca → rickets; iron → anaemia; iodine → goitre; protein → kwashiorkor.</li><li>Canal: mouth → oesophagus → stomach → small intestine → colon → rectum. Accessory: salivary glands, liver (bile), pancreas (enzymes), gall bladder (stores bile).</li><li>Mechanical digestion (teeth, churning) increases surface area; chemical digestion = enzymes breaking LARGE insoluble molecules → SMALL soluble ones: amylase (starch → maltose), protease/pepsin (protein → peptides, stomach pH 2), lipase (fat → fatty acids + glycerol), maltase (maltose → glucose).</li><li>Bile: made in liver, stored in gall bladder, EMULSIFIES fats → larger surface for lipase; ALKALINE → neutralises stomach acid.</li><li><b>Villi:</b> huge surface area, one-cell walls, rich capillaries, lacteal (fats). The universal adaptation formula: SA↑ + thin + moist + transport away.</li><li>Large intestine absorbs water & salts; peristalsis = rhythmic muscle contractions pushing food.</li></ul>"},
{t:"6. Transport in humans",b:"<ul><li>Heart = double pump: RIGHT → pulmonary artery → lungs (deoxygenated); LEFT → aorta → body (oxygenated). Valves prevent backflow; septum separates circulations.</li><li>Cardiac cycle: atria contract → ventricles contract ('lub') → relax ('dub'). Coronary arteries feed the heart muscle; blockage → heart attack.</li><li>Vessels: arteries — thick elastic walls, carry blood AWAY at high pressure; veins — thin walls, VALVES, low pressure return; capillaries — one-cell walls for exchange. 'Artery = away' (pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood!).</li><li>Blood: red cells (biconcave, haemoglobin + iron, no nucleus), white cells (phagocytes engulf; lymphocytes make antibodies), platelets (clotting — fibrin mesh), plasma (transports glucose, CO₂, urea, hormones, heat).</li><li><b>Double circulation advantages:</b> higher pressure to body; separation of oxygenated/deoxygenated blood → efficient O₂ delivery.</li><li>Lymphatic system drains tissue fluid back; lacteals carry fat.</li></ul>"},
{t:"7. Respiration & gas exchange",b:"<ul><li><b>Aerobic:</b> C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + LOTS of energy (mitochondria).</li><li><b>Anaerobic:</b> muscles: glucose → lactic acid + LITTLE energy; oxygen debt = extra O₂ needed afterwards to oxidise lactic acid. Yeast: glucose → ethanol + CO₂ (fermentation — brewing, baking).</li><li>Exercise response: muscles need more energy → more respiration → O₂ demand & CO₂ production rise → brain raises breathing rate/depth and heart rate.</li><li><b>Lungs:</b> trachea → bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli. Alveoli adaptations: millions (huge SA), one-cell walls, moist lining, capillary-wrapped. Exhaled air: more CO₂, less O₂, more water vapour.</li><li>Smoking damages: tar (cancer, mucus), CO (binds haemoglobin), nicotine (addictive). Diseases: bronchitis, emphysema, lung cancer.</li><li>Plants: stomata (guard cells bow with water → open), lenticels, leaf air spaces.</li></ul>"},
{t:"8. Homeostasis & excretion",b:"<ul><li>Homeostasis = keeping internal conditions CONSTANT: temperature, blood glucose, water, ions, urea, CO₂. Negative feedback: change detected → response OPPOSES it → return to set point.</li><li><b>Temperature:</b> hypothalamus detects; sweat evaporation cools; vasodilation/vasoconstriction of skin vessels; shivering generates heat; hairs trap air; behaviour (shade, clothing).</li><li><b>Blood glucose:</b> high → INSULIN → liver/muscles store glucose as glycogen; low → GLUCAGON → glycogen → glucose. Diabetes type 1 (no insulin — injections), type 2 (resistance — diet/exercise). Test: Benedict's.</li><li><b>Kidney:</b> glomerulus filters (water, glucose, salts, urea pass; proteins & cells stay); tubule REABSORBS all glucose, most water, some salts; urea + excess → bladder. ADH controls water reabsorption (less water → more ADH). Dialysis mimics filtration.</li><li>Liver roles: glucose↔glycogen store, deamination (amino acids → urea), alcohol detox, bile production.</li></ul>"},
{t:"9. Coordination & response",b:"<ul><li>Nervous system: CNS (brain + spinal cord) + peripheral nerves. Neurones: sensory, relay, motor. Impulses travel electrically along axons; synapses work chemically (neurotransmitter, ~1 ms delay).</li><li><b>Reflex arc:</b> receptor → sensory neurone → relay (CNS) → motor neurone → effector. FAST, AUTOMATIC, PROTECTIVE.</li><li><b>Eye:</b> cornea (refracts), iris/pupil (light control), lens (focuses via ciliary muscles — fat for near, thin for distant), retina (rods: dim/B-W; cones: colour, at fovea), optic nerve. Short sight → diverging lens; long sight → converging lens.</li><li><b>Hormones vs nerves:</b> nerves fast/short/precise; hormones slow/long/widespread. Both coordinate.</li><li>Plant tropisms: shoots +phototropism/−gravitropism; roots +gravitropism/+hydrotropism. Auxin accumulates on shaded side → cells elongate → bend toward light. Gibberellins (stem elongation), ethene (ripening), abscisic acid (stomatal closing, dormancy). Uses: rooting powder, weedkillers, ripening chambers.</li></ul>"},
{t:"10. Reproduction & inheritance",b:"<ul><li>Asexual: one parent, no gametes, identical clones (bacteria fission, yeast budding, runners, tubers). Fast; preserves good traits; no variation.</li><li>Sexual: gametes fuse (fertilisation) → zygote → VARIED offspring. Variation from meiosis (random assortment, crossing over), random fertilisation, environment.</li><li>DNA double helix in chromosomes; gene = DNA section for a protein; alleles = alternative forms. Dominant expressed in one copy (capital).</li><li><b>Cross method:</b> parental genotypes → gametes (circle) → Punnett square → genotype ratio → phenotype ratio. Monohybrid F₂ = 3:1. Sex determination: XX/XY; P(boy) = ½.</li><li>Mutation: random DNA change — mostly harmful, occasionally beneficial (basis of evolution).</li><li>Plant reproduction: anther → pollen; stigma/ovary; insect vs wind pollination; fertilisation → seed + fruit (from ovary); dispersal; germination needs water, oxygen, suitable temperature.</li><li>Human: FSH (follicle maturation), LH (ovulation), oestrogen/progesterone (lining; the Pill inhibits FSH). Placenta: exchange + barrier — blood never mixes; umbilical cord; amnion.</li></ul>"},
{t:"11. Ecology & the environment",b:"<ul><li>Ecosystem = community + habitat. Food chains: arrows = ENERGY direction (grass → grasshopper → frog → snake). Producers → primary consumers (herbivores) → secondary/tertiary (carnivores).</li><li>~10% energy passes between levels (lost as heat, movement, uneaten parts) → chains rarely exceed 5 levels.</li><li>Pyramids of numbers (can invert — one tree, many insects), biomass (usually upright), energy.</li><li>Carbon cycle: photosynthesis removes CO₂; respiration, combustion, decay return it. Decomposers recycle nutrients. Fossil fuel burning → CO₂ rise → greenhouse effect → global warming.</li><li>Nitrogen cycle: nitrogen-fixing bacteria (legume root nodules, lightning, Haber) → nitrates; nitrifying bacteria (ammonium → nitrates); denitrifying (nitrates → N₂); decomposers (protein → ammonia).</li><li>Eutrophication chain: fertiliser runoff → algal bloom → light blocked → plants die → decomposers multiply → O₂ depleted → fish die. Pesticides bioaccumulate/biomagnify (DDT).</li><li>Population growth: sigmoid curve — lag → exponential → plateau at carrying capacity K. Limiting factors: food, disease, space.</li></ul>"},
{t:"12. Human influences & biotechnology",b:"<ul><li>Bacteria in food: yoghurt (Lactobacillus: lactose → lactic acid, clots milk), cheese (rennet + bacteria), vinegar (ethanol → acetic acid), bread (yeast CO₂).</li><li>Genetic engineering outline: extract human insulin gene → insert into bacterial plasmid → bacteria clone & express it. Cheap, pure, ethical.</li><li>Drug development: modelling, cell tests, animal trials, double-blind clinical trials, peer review. Antibiotics kill bacteria only (not viruses); overuse → resistant strains (MRSA) — finish the course.</li><li>Agriculture: selective breeding (choose parents with desired traits over generations), fertilisers, pesticides, mechanisation vs organic trade-offs.</li></ul>"},
{t:"13. Exam technique for 5090",b:"<ul><li>Command words: 'state' = one fact; 'describe' = sequence; 'explain' = mechanism with 'because/so that'; 'compare' = both sides; 'suggest' = apply to a novel situation.</li><li>Every 'adaptation' answer: feature → therefore → benefit ('villi increase surface area → faster absorption').</li><li>Practical questions (P6): variables (independent/dependent/controlled ×3), method, expected results + conclusion, evaluation (repeats & mean, insulation, control group).</li><li>Food test colours: Benedict's brick-red · iodine blue-black · biuret purple · ethanol milky.</li><li>Drawings: pencil, no shading, ruler labels, correct proportions; plan diagrams show NO individual cells.</li><li>Definitions: diffusion, osmosis, active transport, photosynthesis, respiration, homeostasis, negative feedback, trophic level, allele, dominant, variation, excretion vs egestion, transpiration, peristalsis.</li></ul>"},
{t:"14. Definitions drill",b:"<ul><li><b>Diffusion:</b> net movement of particles from higher to lower concentration down a gradient. <b>Osmosis:</b> diffusion of water through a partially permeable membrane from dilute to more concentrated solution. <b>Active transport:</b> movement against a gradient using energy.</li><li><b>Photosynthesis:</b> green plants make glucose from CO₂ and water using light energy. <b>Respiration:</b> release of energy from glucose in living cells.</li><li><b>Homeostasis:</b> maintaining constant internal conditions despite external changes. <b>Negative feedback:</b> response counteracting a change to restore the set point.</li><li><b>Allele:</b> alternative form of a gene. <b>Dominant:</b> expressed with one copy. <b>Variation:</b> differences between individuals of a species.</li><li><b>Trophic level:</b> feeding level in a food chain. <b>Transpiration:</b> loss of water vapour from leaves driving water up xylem.</li><li><b>Excretion:</b> removal of metabolic waste (urea, CO₂). <b>Egestion:</b> removal of undigested food — NOT excretion.</li></ul>"}],
"olevel/math4024":[
{t:"1. Numbers & accuracy",b:"<ul><li>Types: natural, integers, rational, irrational (√2, π), real. Prime = exactly two factors; 1 not prime; 2 only even prime.</li><li>HCF/LCM by prime factors: HCF = lowest powers of common primes; LCM = highest powers of all primes. HCF × LCM = a × b.</li><li>Fractions ⇄ decimals ⇄ percentages; % change = change/original × 100 — original is the base. Reverse %: divide by the multiplier.</li><li>Ratio sharing: total parts → one part → shares. Bounds: value ± half the rounding unit (upper exclusive). Combining: × → U×U/L×L; ÷ → U÷L/L÷U.</li><li>Standard form a × 10ⁿ (1 ≤ a < 10). Indices: aᵐaⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ; a⁰ = 1; a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ; a^(m/n) = (ⁿ√a)ᵐ. Surds: combine like terms only; rationalise by × surd/surd.</li><li>Estimate: 1 s.f. everything, then compute.</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Algebra I — manipulation & equations",b:"<ul><li>Expansion: FOIL; (a±b)² = a² ± 2ab + b². Factorising: HCF first, then ×→c +→b pair; a²−b² = (a−b)(a+b).</li><li>Linear equations: same operation both sides; clear fractions by ×LCM. Word problems: define → form → solve → answer in words with units.</li><li>Simultaneous: elimination (opposite signs → add; same → subtract); substitution when a lone variable exists. Verify in BOTH equations.</li><li>Inequalities: flip the sign when × or ÷ by a negative. Number line: open < >, closed ≤ ≥.</li><li>Sequences: nth term = dn + (a₁ − d); quadratic sequences have constant second differences.</li><li>Variation: direct y = kx, inverse y = k/x; find k, then use. Rates: speed, density, pressure.</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Algebra II — quadratics & graphs",b:"<ul><li>Solve: factorise (try first) → formula (signs!) → completing the square (x + b/2)² − (b/2)².</li><li>Discriminant Δ = b² − 4ac: >0 two roots, =0 repeated (touch), <0 none. 'Equal roots' → Δ = 0 → solve for the parameter.</li><li>Root relationships: sum = −b/a, product = c/a — build equations from roots and CHECK solutions.</li><li>Parabola: c = y-intercept; axis x = −b/2a; vertex from completing the square; a > 0 minimum. Sketch: roots + intercept + vertex.</li><li>Lines: gradient m = Δy/Δx; y = mx + c; point–gradient form; parallel m equal; perpendicular m₂ = −1/m₁; midpoint & distance.</li><li>Travel graphs: d–t gradient = speed; s–t gradient = acceleration, AREA = distance.</li><li>Functions: f(x); composite fg(x) — INSIDE first; inverse: make x the subject.</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Geometry",b:"<ul><li>Angle facts: line 180°, point 360°, vertically opposite equal; parallels: alternate (Z) equal, corresponding (F) equal, co-interior (C) 180°. Triangle sum 180°.</li><li>Polygons: exterior sum 360°; regular exterior = 360/n; interior = 180 − exterior; interior sum = (n−2)×180°.</li><li>Congruence: SSS, SAS, ASA, RHS. Similarity: lengths ×k, areas ×k², volumes ×k³.</li><li><b>Circle theorems (six):</b> semicircle 90°; centre = 2 × circumference (same arc); same segment equal; cyclic quad opposite 180°; tangent ⊥ radius; alternate segment. Equal radii → isosceles triangles; NAME the theorem as your reason.</li><li>Constructions: perpendicular bisector, angle bisector, 60°/90° — leave ALL arcs visible.</li><li>Loci: perpendicular bisector (two points), angle bisector (two lines), circle (fixed distance). Shade regions.</li></ul>"},
{t:"5. Mensuration",b:"<ul><li>Area: triangle ½bh, parallelogram bh, trapezium ½(a+b)h, circle πr²; semicircle perimeter = πr + 2r (add the diameter!).</li><li>Sector = θ/360 of circle: arc = (θ/360)2πr, area = (θ/360)πr²; segment = sector − triangle.</li><li>Volume: prism (cross-section × length), cylinder πr²h, cone ⅓πr²h, sphere ⁴⁄₃πr³, pyramid ⅓ × base × h. 1 L = 1000 cm³; 1 m³ = 1000 L.</li><li>Surface: cylinder 2πrh + 2πr²; cone πrl + πr², l = √(r² + h²); sphere 4πr²; composites share NO internal faces.</li><li>Similar solids: length k → area k² → volume k³.</li></ul>"},
{t:"6. Trigonometry",b:"<ul><li>Pythagoras; 3D diagonal d² = a² + b² + c².</li><li>SOH CAH TOA; inverses find angles; check DEG mode; exact values 30/45/60.</li><li>Elevation = depression (alternate angles); draw the horizontal FIRST.</li><li><b>Bearings:</b> three figures, clockwise from North; reverse ± 180°; North line at EVERY point; north = d cos θ, east = d sin θ.</li><li>Sine rule a/sinA = b/sinB — needs an opposite pair; ambiguous case: check θ and 180°−θ.</li><li>Cosine rule — SSS or SAS. Keep 4 s.f. in working.</li><li>Area = ½ab sinC (included angle). Sketch first, always.</li></ul>"},
{t:"7. Vectors & transformations",b:"<ul><li>Column vectors add component-wise; magnitude √(x² + y²). AB = b − a; midpoint (a+b)/2.</li><li>Proofs: collinear ⇔ scalar multiple AND a shared point. Parallel ⇔ multiple.</li><li>Transformations — describe FULLY: reflection (mirror line), rotation (centre + angle + direction), translation (column vector), enlargement (SF + centre).</li><li>Negative enlargement passes through the centre; 0 < k < 1 shrinks; area × k², volume × k³.</li><li>Unknown transformation: same size → translation; flipped → perpendicular bisector; resized → k from lengths, centre from rays.</li></ul>"},
{t:"8. Statistics",b:"<ul><li>Sampling: random (least bias), systematic, stratified (proportional), quota, convenience (most biased).</li><li>Averages: mean = Σx/n; median = middle of ORDERED data; mode = most frequent. Mean outlier-sensitive; median robust; mode for categories.</li><li>Grouped data: estimated mean Σfx/Σf with midpoints; modal class = highest bar.</li><li>Cumulative frequency: plot (upper boundary, running total); smooth curve; median at n/2, quartiles n/4 & 3n/4; IQR = UQ − LQ.</li><li>Histograms: frequency DENSITY = frequency ÷ class width; area = frequency.</li><li>Probability: complement 1 − P(A); trees: multiply along, add across; without replacement updates counts; 'at least one' → 1 − P(none); Venn: P(A∪B) = P(A)+P(B)−P(A∩B); conditional P(A|B) = P(A∩B)/P(B).</li></ul>"},
{t:"9. Problem-solving exam strategy",b:"<ul><li>4024 P1: 24 short questions, 2 h → ~5 min each. P2: 10 structured; parts chain — error carried forward credited, NEVER blank a later part.</li><li>Show every line of working — method marks are the currency; correct answer without working can score ZERO.</li><li>3 s.f. final answers; keep 4+ s.f. intermediate; units on answers; money 2 d.p.</li><li>Diagram questions: redraw and label — most geometry/trig errors die at the sketch.</li><li>'Show that': treat the given answer as untouchable; derive it line by line.</li><li>Calculator: DEG mode; bracket negatives; write intermediates before rounding.</li><li>10-minute review: missed parts ('perimeter AND area'), sign sanity, requested form (standard form, exact surd, simplest ratio).</li></ul>"},
{t:"10. Definitions & formula drills",b:"<ul><li><b>HCF/LCM:</b> highest common factor / lowest common multiple. <b>Irrational:</b> not writable as a fraction. <b>Standard form:</b> a × 10ⁿ, 1 ≤ a < 10.</li><li><b>Gradient:</b> change in y ÷ change in x. <b>Discriminant:</b> b² − 4ac, decides real roots.</li><li><b>Hypotenuse:</b> side opposite the right angle. <b>Bearing:</b> clockwise from North, three figures.</li><li><b>Median:</b> middle of ordered data. <b>IQR:</b> upper − lower quartile. <b>Mutually exclusive:</b> cannot happen together.</li><li><b>Enlargement:</b> scaling by k about a centre. <b>Similar:</b> equal angles, proportional sides.</li></ul>"}],
"olevel/isl2058":[
{t:"1. The articles of faith & pillars",b:"<ul><li><b>Articles (Iman):</b> Allah (Tawhid — no partners), angels (Jibril — revelation; Mika'il — provision; Israfil — trumpet; Izra'il — death; Kiraman Katibin — recorders), revealed books (Suhuf Ibrahim, Tawrah, Zabur, Injil, Qur'an — final & preserved), prophets (25 named; final = Muhammad ﷺ), the Last Day, divine decree Qadar.</li><li><b>Pillars:</b> Shahadah (foundation) · Salah (5 daily; wudu; Jumu'ah; rak'ah: Fajr 2, Maghrib 3, others 4) · Zakah (2.5% above nisab held one lunar year; recipients per At-Tawbah 60) · Sawm (Ramadan; exceptions: illness, travel, pregnancy; fidya/kaffara) · Hajj (once if able: ihram, tawaf, sa'i, Arafat — the essential rite, Muzdalifah, Mina, sacrifice, farewell tawaf).</li><li>Compare: which pillar is daily/monthly/annual/lifetime; which tests wealth vs patience.</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. The Qur'an & its teachings",b:"<ul><li>Revelation: began in Cave Hira (610 CE) via Jibril; 23 years; Arabic; 'Qur'an' = recitation.</li><li><b>Compilation:</b> memorised + fragments in the Prophet's life; Abu Bakr ordered collection after Yamamah (632); Uthman standardised the codex and sent copies to the provinces — preventing division.</li><li>Structure: 114 surahs (86 Makkan — short, tawhid-focused; 28 Madinan — law & society), 6236 verses, 30 juz'. Longest: Al-Baqarah; first revealed: 'Iqra' (Al-Alaq).</li><li>Themes to quote: Tawhid (Ikhlas 112), Yusuf (12 — patience & forgiveness), the Hereafter (Ya-Sin 36), parents (17:23), unity (3:103).</li><li>Tajwid preserves recitation; translations convey meanings, not the Qur'an itself.</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Hadith & the Sunnah",b:"<ul><li><b>Hadith</b> = saying/action/approval of the Prophet ﷺ; <b>Sunnah</b> = his way of life. Second authority — explains the Qur'an (how to pray, zakah details, character).</li><li>Structure: isnad (chain) + matn (text). Collections: Sahih Bukhari & Sahih Muslim most authentic, then Abu Dawud, Tirmidhi, Nasa'i, Ibn Majah.</li><li>Scholars graded narrators for truthfulness, memory, continuity — fabricated (mawdu') reports rejected, preserving purity.</li><li>Key hadith: 'Actions are by intentions' (Bukhari 1) · 'None of you believes until he loves for his brother what he loves for himself' · 'The best of you are the best in character' · 'Seeking knowledge is obligatory upon every Muslim'.</li><li>Sunnah never contradicts the Qur'an — it elaborates it.</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. The Prophet's life — Makkan period",b:"<ul><li>Born Makkah 570 CE (Year of the Elephant), Banu Hashim (Quraysh); father Abdullah died before birth; mother Aminah died when he was 6; raised by grandfather Abdul Muttalib, then uncle Abu Talib.</li><li>Known as Al-Amin (the trustworthy); married Khadijah (first believer) at 25; settled the Black Stone dispute at 35.</li><li>First revelation 610 CE in Cave Hira; 3 years of secret preaching, then openly: worship Allah, reject false gods.</li><li>Persecution: 3-year boycott of Banu Hashim; deaths of Khadijah & Abu Talib (Year of Sorrow, 619); Taif rejection (stoned — answered with compassion); Isra & Miraj (621 — five prayers gifted).</li><li>Pledges of Aqabah (621–622): 12 then 75 men & 2 women from Yathrib pledged faith & protection.</li></ul>"},
{t:"5. The Madinan period & the Hijrah",b:"<ul><li><b>Hijrah 622 CE</b> (Year 1 of the Islamic calendar): the Prophet ﷺ & Abu Bakr hid in Cave Thawr 3 days; Ali slept as decoy; arrival at Quba — Masjid Quba; the camel's choice fixed the mosque site in Yathrib (Madinah).</li><li><b>Constitution of Madinah:</b> one ummah — Muslims, Jews & pagans: mutual defence, religious freedom, sanctuary. First written constitution of a plural state.</li><li>Masjid Nabawi: prayer, education, court, government. <b>Mu'akhat:</b> each Muhajir paired with an Ansari — shared homes & wealth.</li><li><b>Battles:</b> Badr (624 — 313 vs 1000, divine help, Qur'an 3:123) · Uhud (625 — archers left the pass; lesson: obedience) · Khandaq (627 — trench by Salman al-Farsi) · Hudaybiyyah (628 — 10-year peace; 'clear victory' 48:1; da'wah opened) · Conquest of Makkah (630 — general amnesty, idols destroyed) · Hunayn & Tabuk.</li><li>Farewell Sermon (632, Arafat): sanctity of life & property, riba forbidden, women's rights, Muslim brotherhood. The Prophet ﷺ passed away 632, aged 63, after appointing Abu Bakr to lead prayer.</li></ul>"},
{t:"6. The Rightly-Guided Caliphs",b:"<ul><li><b>Abu Bakr (632–634), as-Siddiq:</b> Ridda wars (Musaylamah defeated by Khalid ibn Walid), false prophets crushed, zakah refusers fought; ordered the Qur'an's compilation.</li><li><b>Umar (634–644), al-Farooq:</b> conquests — Persia, Syria, Egypt; Jerusalem surrendered to him personally. Administration: diwan, hijri calendar, Bait ul-Mal, police, canals, new cities (Kufa, Basra, Fustat). Martyred 644.</li><li><b>Uthman (644–656), Dhun-Nurayn:</b> standard Qur'anic codex to the provinces; first navy; North Africa, Armenia, Cyprus. Unrest over appointments; martyred 656 while reading the Qur'an.</li><li><b>Ali (656–661):</b> Camel & Siffin (arbitration); Khawarij emerged; capital Kufa; martyred 661 by Ibn Muljam.</li><li>Assessment: unprecedented just expansion; covenant of Umar protected Jerusalem's Christians; administration copied by later empires.</li></ul>"},
{t:"7. Sharia & the sources of law",b:"<ul><li><b>Qur'an</b> — primary; ~500 legal verses (ibadat & mu'amalat).</li><li><b>Sunnah</b> — details: prayer postures, zakah rates, prohibitions. Qur'an commands prayer; Sunnah shows HOW.</li><li><b>Ijma'</b> — consensus; 'My ummah never agrees on error'. Examples: Qur'an's collection, apostasy rulings.</li><li><b>Qiyas</b> — analogy on shared effective cause (illah): wine's prohibition extends to all intoxicants.</li><li>Subsidiary: istihsan, maslahah mursalah (public interest), urf (custom), istishab.</li><li>Schools: Hanafi, Maliki, Shafi'i, Hanbali (Sunni), Ja'fari (Shia) — 'difference of opinion is a mercy' within the framework.</li></ul>"},
{t:"8. Islam in the modern world & ethics",b:"<ul><li><b>Riba:</b> money must not breed money without risk. Modern answers: murabahah, mudarabah, musharakah, ijara, sukuk; ethical screening (no alcohol, gambling, weapons).</li><li><b>Family:</b> marriage = contract with consent & mahr; 'the best of you is best to his family'; kindness to parents (17:23 — say not even 'uff'); children's rights; divorce permitted but 'most hated of permitted things'.</li><li><b>Business:</b> honest weights (Mutaffifin), disclose defects, honour contracts, avoid gharar & maysir.</li><li><b>Society:</b> 'no superiority of Arab over non-Arab'; sanctity of life (5:32); sadaqah & waqf (Islam invented the charitable trust); neighbour rights; hospitality.</li><li><b>Contemporary:</b> organ donation permitted by many scholars (necessities permit prohibitions); genetic engineering for cure, not 'playing God'; verify before sharing news (49:6); jihad = primarily spiritual striving; terrorism condemned — 'whoever kills an innocent kills humanity'.</li><li><b>Interfaith:</b> People of the Book under covenant protection; shared prophets Abraham, Moses, Jesus (Isa — miracle-worker by Allah's leave, not divine).</li></ul>"},
{t:"9. P1 knowledge-answer bank",b:"<ul><li><b>Order of events:</b> first revelation 610 · public preaching 613 · boycott 616–619 · Isra Miraj 621 · pledges of Aqabah 621/622 · Hijrah 622 · Badr 624 · Uhud 625 · Khandaq 627 · Hudaybiyyah 628 · conquest of Makkah 630 · Farewell Sermon & passing 632.</li><li><b>Describe templates:</b> the Hijrah (reasons: assassination plot, Yathrib invitation; events: cave, Quba, constitution, brotherhood; results: Islamic state, calendar begins). Badr (causes, angels 3:123, effects: prestige). Hudaybiyyah (terms: 10-year truce, return this year, Umrah next; significance: da'wah flourished, Khalid & Amr embraced Islam).</li><li><b>Caliphs table:</b> Abu Bakr — ridda, compilation; Umar — conquests, diwan, calendar; Uthman — codex, navy; Ali — Kufa, arbitration lessons.</li><li><b>Personalities:</b> Khadijah, Abu Bakr, Umar (justice), Uthman, Ali, Bilal (equality), Salman (trench), Zayd ibn Thabit (compiler).</li></ul>"},
{t:"10. P2 application-answer bank",b:"<ul><li>Every application answer: quote a verse/hadith WITH source → why it matters → 3 concrete steps → conclusion. Four layers.</li><li><b>Cheating:</b> 'Whoever deceives us is not one of us' (Muslim) · long-term harm: knowledge without barakah, corrupt character · advice: prepare early, Allah sees, seek help, make dua.</li><li><b>Disrespectful child:</b> 17:23 + 'Paradise lies under the mother's feet' (Nasa'i) · speak gently, help with chores, make dua, never abandon them in old age.</li><li><b>Business fraud:</b> 'The truthful merchant is with the prophets, the truthful and the martyrs' (Tirmidhi) · disclose defects, fair scales, accept returns — trust brings barakah.</li><li><b>Interest temptation:</b> 2:278–279 declares war on riba · seek scholarships, family help, halal financing, part-time work.</li><li><b>Social media rumour:</b> 'Enough sin to repeat all you hear' (Abu Dawud) + 49:6 verification · pause, check sources, speak good or stay silent.</li></ul>"},
{t:"11. Exam technique",b:"<ul><li>P1 rewards SEQUENCE and DETAIL: dates, names, places, numbers. Learn timelines as chains.</li><li>'Describe' = narrate in order with specifics; 'Explain the importance' = effects → significance. 4 marks ≈ 4 developed points.</li><li>P2: read the scenario twice; identify the teaching (tawhid, honesty, kindness, justice, patience); weave the scenario details in — never answer generically.</li><li>Memorise 15–20 short quotes with sources — sourced quotes score higher.</li><li>Two-sided questions: Islamic ruling first → modern context → practical halal alternative → committed conclusion.</li><li>Structure: intro naming the theme, paragraphs per teaching, conclusion sentence.</li></ul>"}]
};
